1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,480
You scan a paper square at a
noodle stall in Bali,

2
00:00:03,020 --> 00:00:04,900
and your bank app has
never met this stall.

3
00:00:05,700 --> 00:00:07,640
Two seconds later, the
noodles are paid for.

4
00:00:08,833 --> 00:00:13,909
That square is called QRIS, and almost
seventy-two million people in Indonesia

5
00:00:13,909 --> 00:00:14,744
pay with it.

6
00:00:14,744 --> 00:00:16,992
Fifty-one million shops accept it.

7
00:00:16,992 --> 00:00:21,613
In October, transactions were up
seventy-one percent on a year earlier.

8
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:25,640
So how does one sticker
work with every app?

9
00:00:26,200 --> 00:00:28,400
And how did paying this way
start working in Bangkok,

10
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:29,260
Tokyo, and Seoul?

11
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:31,408
Go back to 2019.

12
00:00:31,408 --> 00:00:35,261
Bank Indonesia counted twenty-six
companies running QR payments,

13
00:00:35,261 --> 00:00:36,800
each with its own format.

14
00:00:37,880 --> 00:00:41,100
A shop that wanted every customer had
to put up a code for each one.

15
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:44,500
Scan with the wrong app, and
the code meant nothing.

16
00:00:45,613 --> 00:00:49,233
Think of each app as a plug and
each shop's code as a socket.

17
00:00:50,133 --> 00:00:53,753
Every app had its own plug shape, so
every shop needed a wall of different

18
00:00:53,853 --> 00:00:54,293
sockets.

19
00:00:55,413 --> 00:00:58,835
On August seventeenth, 2019,
Indonesia's Independence Day,

20
00:00:58,835 --> 00:01:03,011
the central bank and the payment
industry launched one socket shape for

21
00:01:03,011 --> 00:01:03,591
everyone.

22
00:01:03,591 --> 00:01:04,809
They called it QRIS.

23
00:01:05,967 --> 00:01:09,202
Many people think Indonesia's QRIS
code is an app you download.

24
00:01:09,202 --> 00:01:12,942
It's a rulebook for how the code is
written and how the companies on each

25
00:01:12,942 --> 00:01:13,647
side connect.

26
00:01:14,767 --> 00:01:18,667
From 2020, payment codes at
Indonesian shops had to follow it.

27
00:01:19,547 --> 00:01:23,707
A shop now signs up with one provider,
and any provider's app can pay it.

28
00:01:24,607 --> 00:01:26,627
The printed sticker itself is free.

29
00:01:28,433 --> 00:01:30,340
Now follow one scan, on one map.

30
00:01:30,340 --> 00:01:33,691
Your app sits on the left, the
stall's bank on the right,

31
00:01:33,691 --> 00:01:35,771
and in the middle there's a switch.

32
00:01:36,913 --> 00:01:38,933
A switch is the wiring behind the wall.

33
00:01:39,533 --> 00:01:42,973
It's a company that passes payment
messages between providers that aren't

34
00:01:43,053 --> 00:01:44,353
wired to each other directly.

35
00:01:45,487 --> 00:01:48,407
When your camera reads the
square, it reads plain text.

36
00:01:49,347 --> 00:01:52,667
Inside is which shop this is, which
provider holds its account,

37
00:01:53,247 --> 00:01:55,047
and a country code, Indonesia.

38
00:01:56,387 --> 00:01:59,647
The amount isn't in there, which is why
the sticker never has to change.

39
00:02:00,787 --> 00:02:03,507
You type fifty thousand
rupiah and your PIN.

40
00:02:03,507 --> 00:02:07,497
Your app checks your balance, then
sends a message to the switch:

41
00:02:07,497 --> 00:02:10,761
take fifty thousand from this
person, for this stall.

42
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,720
The switch reads which provider holds the
stall's account and forwards the

43
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:16,340
message there.

44
00:02:17,340 --> 00:02:19,600
The stall's bank checks the
account and says yes.

45
00:02:20,680 --> 00:02:24,240
A tick appears on your phone, and the
owner's phone chimes on the counter.

46
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:28,220
The owner sees the amount and the time
without touching a card machine.

47
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:32,460
Providers build that whole round trip to
finish while you're still holding

48
00:02:32,500 --> 00:02:32,960
your phone.

49
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:36,885
But who pays for those two seconds?

50
00:02:36,885 --> 00:02:41,560
In our card video, a hundred-dollar tap cost
the shop two dollars seventy-five.

51
00:02:42,733 --> 00:02:45,413
The fee a shop pays to accept
a payment has a name,

52
00:02:45,873 --> 00:02:48,213
the merchant discount rate, or MDR.

53
00:02:48,793 --> 00:02:52,353
It's a small slice taken off each sale
before the money reaches the shop.

54
00:02:53,413 --> 00:02:57,900
Since October 2026, a QRIS payment up to
a hundred thousand rupiah costs

55
00:02:57,900 --> 00:02:59,559
most shops nothing at all.

56
00:02:59,559 --> 00:03:03,553
The smallest businesses pay nothing
up to five hundred thousand.

57
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:07,224
Above that, the fee is still
under one percent.

58
00:03:07,224 --> 00:03:09,820
And the shop isn't allowed
to pass it on to you.

59
00:03:10,987 --> 00:03:14,130
So why would anyone in the
middle take almost nothing?

60
00:03:14,130 --> 00:03:16,873
There's one thing to know
about Bank Indonesia:

61
00:03:16,873 --> 00:03:19,673
it wants small shops inside
the digital economy,

62
00:03:19,673 --> 00:03:21,727
and it treats QRIS as their way in.

63
00:03:22,813 --> 00:03:26,433
Every scan also leaves a record of
what a shop sells and how often.

64
00:03:27,453 --> 00:03:30,833
Bank Indonesia wants those records to
help small shops get loans,

65
00:03:31,353 --> 00:03:32,733
even with no credit history.

66
00:03:33,673 --> 00:03:37,513
Three years of daily chimes at a noodle
stall is a sales history a bank

67
00:03:37,573 --> 00:03:38,013
can read.

68
00:03:39,167 --> 00:03:41,007
For the apps, each scan is a visit.

69
00:03:41,807 --> 00:03:45,267
You open them, you see your balance, and
you might stay for the next thing

70
00:03:45,307 --> 00:03:45,767
they sell.

71
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:49,438
So for a
fifty-thousand-rupiah bowl of noodles,

72
00:03:49,438 --> 00:03:51,340
the stall keeps all fifty thousand.

73
00:03:51,340 --> 00:03:55,565
With the cut from our card example, the same
bowl would lose about one thousand

74
00:03:55,565 --> 00:03:56,675
four hundred rupiah.

75
00:03:57,833 --> 00:04:01,110
That's how a printed square
reached fifty-one million shops,

76
00:04:01,110 --> 00:04:03,851
more than one for every six
people in the country.

77
00:04:03,851 --> 00:04:06,053
Nearly all of them are small businesses.

78
00:04:07,147 --> 00:04:11,187
And the switches carrying those messages
are Indonesian companies licensed

79
00:04:11,227 --> 00:04:12,027
by the central bank.

80
00:04:12,807 --> 00:04:15,267
Card payments inside Indonesia
run through them too.

81
00:04:17,153 --> 00:04:19,673
That last part is what the
United States didn't like.

82
00:04:20,780 --> 00:04:25,413
In March 2025, the US trade office put
QRIS in its yearly report on trade

83
00:04:25,413 --> 00:04:26,039
barriers.

84
00:04:26,039 --> 00:04:29,232
It said foreign companies,
American ones included,

85
00:04:29,232 --> 00:04:30,860
weren't consulted enough.

86
00:04:31,967 --> 00:04:35,347
The same report pointed at the rule that
domestic card payments must go

87
00:04:35,387 --> 00:04:36,967
through switches inside Indonesia.

88
00:04:37,967 --> 00:04:39,967
Global card networks can't
run that part there.

89
00:04:41,067 --> 00:04:43,576
Look at who designed that socket.

90
00:04:43,576 --> 00:04:48,890
The socket shape QRIS uses comes from
EMVCo, a standards body set up by

91
00:04:48,890 --> 00:04:51,104
Europay, Mastercard and Visa.

92
00:04:52,200 --> 00:04:54,040
Bank Indonesia's reply was polite.

93
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:57,620
It's open to working with any
country, the US included.

94
00:04:58,733 --> 00:05:01,293
To be fair to the card networks,
they aren't losing the world.

95
00:05:02,333 --> 00:05:06,273
Visa and Mastercard still lead in
Indonesian credit cards and in payments

96
00:05:06,313 --> 00:05:07,093
between countries.

97
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:11,222
Outside a handful of countries,
your QRIS app doesn't work,

98
00:05:11,222 --> 00:05:12,539
and your card still does.

99
00:05:14,367 --> 00:05:18,009
Now the second question: how
does this cross borders?

100
00:05:18,009 --> 00:05:22,460
In August 2022, Thailand became the
first country linked to QRIS,

101
00:05:22,460 --> 00:05:25,087
then Malaysia in 2023, then Singapore.

102
00:05:26,233 --> 00:05:30,913
Japan joined in August 2025, then
South Korea and China in 2026.

103
00:05:31,353 --> 00:05:32,413
That makes six countries.

104
00:05:33,520 --> 00:05:36,585
When a tourist from Thailand pays
a Bali stall with QRIS,

105
00:05:36,585 --> 00:05:38,858
the money is never turned into US dollars.

106
00:05:38,858 --> 00:05:42,400
Most people would guess it gets
converted somewhere in the middle.

107
00:05:43,467 --> 00:05:46,147
That tourist walks up to the
same noodle stall in Bali.

108
00:05:47,027 --> 00:05:50,407
The tourist scans the same sticker
using a Thai banking app.

109
00:05:51,533 --> 00:05:55,093
The Thai app sees that the code is Indonesian
and sends the payment to Thailand's

110
00:05:55,153 --> 00:05:55,473
switch.

111
00:05:56,513 --> 00:05:58,913
That switch passes it across
to Indonesia's switch,

112
00:05:59,293 --> 00:06:02,273
which takes it to the stall's bank
down the same road as before.

113
00:06:03,400 --> 00:06:06,700
Before typing a PIN, the tourist
sees the price in baht.

114
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:11,180
Then the baht balance goes down, and
the stall still receives rupiah.

115
00:06:12,267 --> 00:06:16,747
Behind the scenes, a few appointed banks
in each country swap rupiah and

116
00:06:16,847 --> 00:06:18,147
baht with each other directly.

117
00:06:19,067 --> 00:06:21,627
That final handover between
banks is called settlement,

118
00:06:22,147 --> 00:06:24,347
and here it happens in the
two local currencies.

119
00:06:25,453 --> 00:06:26,233
That's the adapter.

120
00:06:27,053 --> 00:06:30,113
Two countries with different sockets
joined through their switches,

121
00:06:30,473 --> 00:06:31,533
each keeping its own money.

122
00:06:32,353 --> 00:06:33,393
It works the other way too.

123
00:06:34,293 --> 00:06:37,353
In Bangkok, you scan a Thai code
with your Indonesian app,

124
00:06:37,813 --> 00:06:39,573
and your rupiah balance goes down.

125
00:06:41,413 --> 00:06:43,426
And money is mostly flowing in.

126
00:06:43,426 --> 00:06:47,952
Up to July 2026, visitors spent five
point nine trillion rupiah through

127
00:06:47,952 --> 00:06:52,478
QRIS in Indonesia, more than three times
what Indonesians spent with it

128
00:06:52,478 --> 00:06:52,981
abroad.

129
00:06:52,981 --> 00:06:56,565
Most of those visitors come from
Malaysia and Singapore,

130
00:06:56,565 --> 00:06:59,772
paying from ringgit and
Singapore dollar accounts.

131
00:07:00,913 --> 00:07:01,986
It's still small.

132
00:07:01,986 --> 00:07:05,623
When Japan joined, only
thirty-five shops there accepted it,

133
00:07:05,623 --> 00:07:10,213
and only Indonesians could pay that way,
not Japanese visitors to Indonesia.

134
00:07:11,333 --> 00:07:15,033
Next, Bank Indonesia is in talks
with India, Saudi Arabia,

135
00:07:15,273 --> 00:07:16,673
Vietnam, and Australia.

136
00:07:17,573 --> 00:07:22,053
In Saudi Arabia, the idea is to let
Indonesian pilgrims on Hajj and Umrah

137
00:07:22,153 --> 00:07:22,613
pay with it.

138
00:07:23,727 --> 00:07:26,127
And six central banks,
Indonesia's among them,

139
00:07:26,547 --> 00:07:29,187
are building a shared hub for
their instant payment systems.

140
00:07:30,067 --> 00:07:33,267
It's still being built, but one connection
there could reach several countries

141
00:07:33,287 --> 00:07:33,747
at once.

142
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:37,058
So how powerful is QRIS?

143
00:07:37,058 --> 00:07:41,374
At home, it put digital payments in
fifty-one million shops for the price

144
00:07:41,374 --> 00:07:42,600
of a printed square.

145
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:47,700
Abroad, it's an adapter, and still
a small one next to Visa.

146
00:07:48,500 --> 00:07:51,700
Each country plugged in gives the next
tourist one more place to pay.

147
00:07:53,567 --> 00:07:56,587
The stall in Bali still has the same
paper square on its counter.

148
00:07:57,727 --> 00:08:01,347
Now tourists pay it from their own apps,
and the owner just hears the chime.

149
00:08:03,200 --> 00:08:07,500
Next, we follow a bank wire across borders
the old way and see what it actually

150
00:08:07,560 --> 00:08:07,900
sends.
